1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
AI agents already pay invoices. And an agent that can pay can be fooled:

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
a hidden instruction in an invoice, a real vendor on a look-alike domain, an amount that does not fit its task.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
In 2026 an agent sent fifty million tokens when it was asked for four.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
SafePay is the firewall every agent asks before it pays with PayPal.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The operator writes the rules once, in plain words. Nemotron turns them into a policy and shows me every field before it applies.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
I apply it signed with a key this browser created and that never leaves it. From now on nobody changes these rules without that key, not even with server access.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:43,000
A real Notion invoice. Policy in order, the vendor checked on the live web, and the reasoning confirms it serves the task. Paid through the PayPal sandbox, sealed.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The same invoice, but the money goes to a look-alike domain. The vendor directory blocks it without a single model call.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Nine hundred dollars: over the per-payment cap. Hard block.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:03,000
One hundred and twenty dollars to a casino. Inside every limit, and still held: the web found complaints and the reasoning says it does not serve the task of paying invoices. A spending cap never catches this.

11
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
A legitimate cloud invoice, but over the three hundred I asked to be consulted on. It waits for me.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:18,000
It reaches me where I am, one tap and it is paid. And the approval is signed by this browser's key, the same one that signs the rules: a stolen operator token approves nothing.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The last one is blocked not for its amount, but because it would breach the daily cap across the whole burst. SafePay keeps the day's count, not one isolated payment.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
If something smells wrong, one click freezes the agent the way a bank freezes a card: its next payment is refused before any check runs. A PayPal dispute pulls the same switch on its own.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Every decision is a link in a signed hash chain. I tamper with one row and the whole chain fails at once. That is the record an auditor can believe.

16
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:05,000
We measured SafePay against seventeen real attacks, and the benchmark is in the repo. But a published benchmark can be studied.

17
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:13,000
So here is one that cannot: the model is writing six attacks it has never seen, right now, and SafePay judges them live.

18
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Four of six caught. Two got through, and that is shown as it is: we do not curate what the model writes. Every miss comes with the rule that would close it.

19
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:29,000
One click signs those rules with my key, applies them, and runs the same attacks again.

20
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Six of six. Miss, fix and catch, in front of you. That is proof, not promise.

21
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:52,000
SafePay does not need to hold the money to protect it. SafePay Check is a remote MCP server: any agent asks before its own checkout and gets a signed verdict. It is in the official MCP Registry. The same rules apply to PayPal and to stablecoins like PYUSD and USDC.

22
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
SafePay: rules in plain words, the vendor checked on the web, reasoning about every payment, signed approvals and a record nobody edits. pip install safepay. Thank you.
